From 01024f1b2053b0dc7d12d302c184a614a78f21da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:30:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Foundations slice: real sandbox, durable run records, eval
 harness
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the gaps called out in the audit before any GitHub/X/scheduler
work starts:

- sandbox.py: run_command no longer trusts shell=True + cwd as
  isolation. Adds a RestrictedLocalBackend (scrubbed env, POSIX
  rlimits, output caps, enforced timeout — explicitly NOT isolation)
  and a DockerBackend (--network none, memory/pids/cpu caps,
  no-new-privileges, auto-removed container) used only when the
  Docker daemon is actually reachable. select_backend() probes and
  falls back honestly; the chosen backend and its real capabilities
  are recorded with every result, never assumed.

- run_record.py: every run_task() call gets a durable JSON record
  (run id, task, model, ordered tool calls with args/result/error/
  timing, completion check, final status) persisted to runs/.

- forge.py:
  - record_evidence() keeps failed run_command results in
    "failed command execution" / "failed test execution" buckets
    instead of silently dropping them.
  - messages is reset per run_task() call instead of accumulating
    across tasks in the same process (old gap: task N+1 could see
    task N's conversation).
  - TOOLS is now ToolSpec entries (schema, risk tier, sandboxed flag)
    instead of a bare name->function map.
  - run_command routed through sandbox.select_backend().

- eval_harness.py: TaskCase/run_suite/compare_baseline_vs_candidate.
  Runs fixed, reproducible cases (real tools, real completion
  checking, scripted model responses) in an isolated workspace per
  case, and compares two run_task implementations with an explicit
  is_safe_to_promote rule (zero regressions, not just equal-or-better
  aggregate pass rate).

37/37 tests pass (11 pre-existing + 26 new across test_sandbox.py,
test_run_record.py, test_eval_harness.py, and additions to
test_forge.py).

Not in this slice: learning harness proper (turning failures into
regression cases / candidate strategies), persistent memory,
GitHub/X/scheduler integrations. Those build on top of this.

